    "body": "## Summary\n\n- Skip kind 10050 publication when the existing inbox relay event already matches configured relays.\n- Keep the old event ID and `created_at` intact to avoid leaking restart timing.\n- Preserve non-fatal publish behavior when the existing relay event cannot be checked.\n- Add regression coverage using a mock relay.\n\nCloses marmot-protocol/marmot-security#93\n\n## Root Cause\n\nStartup always signed and published a fresh kind 10050 event, so public relays exposed a near-real-time restart timestamp even when the relay list had not changed.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `just ci`\n- `cargo test --features tor`\n- `cargo llvm-cov --all-features --json --output-path /tmp/transponder-pr47-coverage.json`\n- `cargo fmt --check`\n- `cargo check`\n- `cargo clippy -- -D warnings`\n- `cargo test`\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n\n## Summary by CodeRabbit\n\n* **New Features**\n  * Inbox-relay publishing now detects when the configured relay set is unchanged and skips republishing, preventing redundant updates.\n\n* **Tests**\n  * Added test coverage to ensure relay publishing is skipped when configuration matches the most recently published relay list, preserving the original publication metadata.\n\n<!-- end of auto-generated comment: release notes by coderabbit.ai -->\n",
